The [[FIFA Club World Cup]] is an international [[association football]] competition organised by the [[FIFA]], the sport's global governing body.<ref name="Format">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/01/95/45/34/regulationsfcwc2012_e.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130328220018/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/01/95/45/34/regulationsfcwc2012%5fe.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=March 28, 2013 |title=FIFA Club World Cup Japan 2012 – Regulations |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |format=PDF |access-date=11 March 2013 }}</ref> It is the replacement or continuation of the [[Intercontinental Cup (1960–2004)|Intercontinental Cup]]. The championship was first contested as the FIFA Club World Championship in 2000.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/tournament=107/edition=3692/news/newsid=71334.html |title=Brazil 2000 Final Draw |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=14 October 1999 |access-date=6 March 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131109024044/http://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/tournament%3D107/edition%3D3692/news/newsid%3D71334.html |archive-date=9 November 2013 }}</ref> It was not held between [[2001 FIFA Club World Championship|2001]] and 2004 due to a combination of factors, most importantly the collapse of FIFA's marketing partner [[International Sport and Leisure]].<ref name="FIFA 2001">{{cite web|url=http://de.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/tournament=107/edition=4735/releases/newsid=91574.html |title=FIFA decides to postpone 2001 Club World Championship to 2003 |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=18 May 2001 |access-date=6 March 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131109023834/http://de.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/tournament%3D107/edition%3D4735/releases/newsid%3D91574.html |archive-date=9 November 2013 }}</ref> Following a change in format which saw the FIFA Club World Championship absorb the Intercontinental Cup, it was relaunched in 2005, and took its current name the season afterwards.<ref name="Relaunch">{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/tournament=107/edition=4735/releases/newsid=96696.html |title=Toyota confirmed as FIFA Club World Championship 2005 naming partner |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=15 March 2005 |access-date=6 March 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131109024139/http://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/tournament%3D107/edition%3D4735/releases/newsid%3D96696.html |archive-date=9 November 2013 }}</ref>

The current format of the tournament, in use since the competition was revamped ahead of the [[2025 FIFA Club World Cup|2025 edition]], features 32 teams competing for the title at venues within the host nation; 12 teams from [[UEFA|Europe]], 6 from [[CONMEBOL|South America]], 4 from [[Asian Football Confederation|Asia]], 4 from [[Confederation of African Football|Africa]], 4 from [[CONCACAF|North, Central America and Caribbean]], 1 from [[Oceania Football Confederation|Oceania]], and 1 team from the host nation. The teams are drawn into eight groups of four, with each team playing three group stage matches in a [[Round-robin tournament|round-robin]] format. The top two teams from each group advance to the knockout stage, starting with the round of 16 and culminating with the final.<ref name="Format" />

[[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] holds the record for most victories, winning the competition five times since its inception. Teams from Spain have won the tournament the most times, with eight wins produced from that nation. The [[UEFA|Union of European Football Associations]] (UEFA) is the most successful confederation of the competition, with sixteen titles earned by nine of its clubs. 

The current champions are [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], who won their second title following a 3–0 win against [[Paris Saint-Germain F.C.|Paris Saint-Germain]] in the [[2025 FIFA Club World Cup final|2025 final]], held at the [[MetLife Stadium]] in [[East Rutherford, New Jersey]].<ref name="Overview"/>

==History==
[[File:Aerial view of the Maracanã Stadium.jpg|300px|thumb|right|[[Maracanã Stadium|Estádio do Maracanã]] in [[Rio de Janeiro]], [[Brazil]], the location of the first Club World Cup final in [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship Final|2000]].|alt=An aerial view of the Maracanã Stadium in Rio de Janeiro.]]
The first [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship Final|final]] of the competition was an all-[[Campeonato Brasileiro Série A|Brazilian]] affair, as well as the only one which saw one side have home advantage.<ref name="FIFA 2000" /> [[CR Vasco da Gama|Vasco da Gama]] could not take advantage of its local support, being beaten by [[Sport Club Corinthians Paulista|Corinthians]] 4–3 on [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalties]] after a 0–0 draw in [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]].<ref name="BBC">{{cite news| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/special_report/1999/12/99/world_club_championship/604434.stm |work=[[BBC News|British Broadcasting Corporation News]] | title=Corinthians crowned world champions |date=15 January 2000 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> The [[2001 FIFA Club World Championship|second edition]] of the competition was planned for Spain in 2001, and was supposed to feature twelve clubs.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesf/fifa-wcc01.html |title=2001 FIFA Club World Cup |last1=Stokkermans |first1=Karel |website=[[Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation]] |date=31 December 2005 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> However, it was canceled on 18 May, due to a combination of factors, most importantly the collapse of FIFA's marketing partner [[International Sport and Leisure]].<ref name="FIFA 2001" /> FIFA agreed with Toyota to merge the [[Intercontinental Cup (1960–2004)|Toyota Cup]] and Club World Championship into one event.<ref name="Relaunch" />

The [[2005 FIFA Club World Championship|2005 edition]] saw Brazilian club [[São Paulo FC|São Paulo]] pushed to the limit by Saudi side [[Al-Ittihad (Jeddah)|Al-Ittihad]] to reach the [[2005 FIFA Club World Championship Final|final]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/japan2005/news/newsid=102064/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131209154426/http://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/japan2005/news/newsid=102064/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=December 9, 2013 |title=Sao Paulo make the final – but only just |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=15 December 2005 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> In the final, one goal from [[Mineiro (footballer, born 1975)|Mineiro]] was enough to dispatch [[Premier League|English]] club [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]]; Mineiro became the first player to score in a Club World Cup final.<ref name="FIFA 2005" /> [[Sport Club Internacional|Internacional]] defeated defending world and South American champions São Paulo in the [[2006 Copa Libertadores Finals]] in order to qualify for the [[2006 FIFA Club World Cup|2006 tournament]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/japan2006/news/newsid=105073/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131219035923/http://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/japan2006/news/newsid=105073/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=December 19, 2013 |title=Inter take title and a place in Japan |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=17 August 2006 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> In the semi-finals, Internacional beat [[Egyptian Premier League|Egyptian]] side [[Al Ahly SC|Al Ahly]], qualifying for the [[2006 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]] against [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] from [[La Liga|Spain]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/japan2006/matches/round=24830400/match=24830003/index.html |title=Al Ahly Sporting Club – Sport Clube Internacional |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=13 December 2006 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> One late goal from [[Carlos Adriano de Souza Vieira|Adriano Gabiru]] allowed the trophy to be kept in Brazil once again.<ref name="FIFA 2006" />

It was in [[2007 FIFA Club World Cup|2007]] when Brazilian hegemony was finally broken; [[Serie A|Italian]] side [[A.C. Milan|Milan]] disputed a close match against [[J. League|Japan]]'s [[Urawa Red Diamonds]], who were pushed by over 67,000 fans at [[Yokohama]]'s [[International Stadium Yokohama|International Stadium]], and won 1–0 to reach the [[2007 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/japan2007/matches/round=249930/match=61297/index.html |title=Milan set up Boca showdown |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=13 December 2007 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> In the final, Milan defeated [[Argentine Primera División|Argentine]] side [[Boca Juniors]] 4–2, in a match that saw the first player to be [[Ejection (sports)|sent off]] in a Club World Cup final, Milan's [[Kakha Kaladze]] from [[Georgia (country)|Georgia]], in the 77th minute. Eleven minutes later, Boca Juniors' [[Pablo Ledesma]] would join Kaladze as he too was sent off.<ref name="FIFA 2007" /> The [[2008 FIFA Club World Cup|following year]], [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] would emulate Milan by beating their semi-final opponents, Japan's [[Gamba Osaka]], 5–3.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/japan2008/matches/round=252222/match=300073124/index.html |title=United hit five in thriller |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=18 December 2008 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> They saw off [[Ecuadorian Serie A|Ecuadorian]] club [[L.D.U. Quito|LDU Quito]] 1–0 in the [[2008 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]], as the English side became the second European team to win the tournament.<ref name="FIFA 2008" />

Barcelona dethroned world and European champions Manchester United in the [[2009 UEFA Champions League Final]] to qualify for the [[2009 FIFA Club World Cup|2009 edition]] of the Club World Cup.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/uae2009/news/newsid=1063207/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130802071424/http://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/uae2009/news/newsid=1063207/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=August 2, 2013 |title=Barça reign supreme |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=27 May 2009 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> Barcelona defeated [[Liga MX|Mexican]] club [[Atlante F.C.|Atlante]] 3–1 in the semi-finals and met [[Estudiantes de La Plata|Estudiantes]] from Argentina in the [[2009 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/uae2009/matches/round=252911/match=300111968/index.html |title=Exceptional Barça reach final |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=16 December 2009 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> After a very close encounter which saw the need for extra time, [[Lionel Messi]] scored from a header to snatch victory for Barcelona and complete an unprecedented [[List of association football teams to have won four or more trophies in one season|sextuple]] (six trophies in a calendar year).<ref name="FIFA 2009" /><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.marca.com/2012/12/19/futbol/equipos/barcelona/1355909675.html |title= Recordando la temporada perfecta |trans-title=Remembering a perfect season |last1=Liceras |first1=Ángel |work=[[Marca (newspaper)|MARCA]] |date=19 December 2012 |access-date=6 March 2013 | language=es}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url = http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/8422908.stm | title = Barcelona beat Estudiantes to win the Club World Cup |work=[[BBC Sport|British Broadcasting Corporation Sport]] |date=19 December 2012 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldfootball/news/newsid=1151676.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091231034611/http://www.fifa.com/worldfootball/news/newsid=1151676.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=December 31, 2009 |title=The year in pictures |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> The [[2010 FIFA Club World Cup|2010 edition]] saw the first non-European and non-South American side to reach the final: [[Linafoot|Congo]]'s [[TP Mazembe|Mazembe]] defeated Brazil's Internacional 2–0 in the semi-finals to set up a [[2010 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]] with Italian [[Inter Milan|Internazionale]], who had beaten [[K. League|South Korean]] club [[Seongnam Ilhwa Chunma]] 3–0.<ref name="fifa">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/uae2010/matches/round=254486/match=300140539/index.html |title=Inter stunned as Mazembe reach final |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=14 December 2010 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/uae2010/matches/round=254486/match=300140535/index.html |title=Seongnam sunk as Inter stroll |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=15 December 2010 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> Internazionale would go on to beat Mazembe by the same scoreline to win their fifth title of the year.<ref name="FIFA 2010" />

In [[2011 FIFA Club World Cup|2011]], Barcelona would once again show its class after winning their semi-final match 4–0 against [[Qatar Stars League|Qatari]] club [[Al Sadd SC|Al-Sadd]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/japan2011/matches/round=257435/match=300180906/index.html |title=Adriano at the double as Barça cruise|publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=15 December 2011 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> In the [[2011 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]], Barcelona would win by the same scoreline against Brazilian side [[Santos FC|Santos]]; this is, to date, the largest final winning margin by any victor of the competition.<ref name="FIFA 2011" /> The [[2012 FIFA Club World Cup|2012 edition]] saw Europe's dominance come to an end, as Corinthians traveled to Japan to join Barcelona in becoming two-time winners of the competition.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/newsid=1973250/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121221123049/http://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/newsid=1973250/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=December 21, 2012 |title=Corinthians halt European domination |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=17 December 2012 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> In the semi-finals, [[Al Ahly SC|Al Ahly]] managed to keep the scoreline close as Corinthians' [[Paolo Guerrero]] scored to send the ''Timão'' into their second final.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/matches/round=259325/match=300219242/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121214081505/http://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/matches/round=259325/match=300219242/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=December 14, 2012 |title=Corinthians edge Al-Ahly to reach final |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=12 December 2012 |access-date=6 March 2013 }}</ref> Guerrero would once again come through for Corinthians in the [[2012 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]] as the ''Timão'' saw off English side [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] 1–0 in order to bring the trophy back to Brazil.<ref name="FIFA 2012" />
{{clear}}

==List of finals==
The [[Nissan Stadium (Yokohama)|International Stadium Yokohama]] in Japan has played host to the FIFA Club World Cup final the most times, with eight title-deciding matches held.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/destination/stadiums/stadium=5002366/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112173950/http://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/destination/stadiums/stadium=5002366/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=12 November 2013 |title=International Stadium Yokohama |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association|access-date=4 March 2013}}</ref> Along with the [[Maracanã Stadium|Estádio do Maracanã]], they are the only venues in the world to have hosted both the [[List of FIFA World Cup finals|FIFA World Cup final]] and the FIFA Club World Cup final (International Stadium Yokohama hosted the [[2002 FIFA World Cup final]] while the deciding match of the [[Uruguay v Brazil (1950 FIFA World Cup)|1950 FIFA World Cup]] was disputed at the Maracanã).<ref name="Yokohama">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/destination/stadiums/stadium=5002366/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112173950/http://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/destination/stadiums/stadium=5002366/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=12 November 2013 |title=International Stadium Yokohama|publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association|access-date=4 March 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/stadiums/stadium=214/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080219080052/http://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/stadiums/stadium=214/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=19 February 2008 |title=Maracanã – Estádio Jornalista Mário Filho |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association|access-date=4 March 2013}}</ref> The [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship final]] remains the highest attended final of the competition, with 73,000 fans attending the all-Brazilian match.<ref name="FIFA 2000" /> The final was also the only one which saw two clubs from the same nation dispute it.<ref name="FIFA 2000" /> The [[2021 FIFA Club World Cup final|2021 final]] had the fewest spectators, with 32,871 (not including the [[2020 FIFA Club World Cup final|2020 final]], which had restricted seating due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]]).

The [[2007 FIFA Club World Cup final|2007 final]] holds the record for most goals scored in regulation in a Club World Cup final, with six goals scored by five players, while the 2000 final remains the only scoreless decider.<ref name="FIFA 2000" /><ref name="FIFA 2007" /> The [[2011 FIFA Club World Cup final|2011 final]] became the most lopsided match of the competition, with the triumphant team winning by a difference of four goals,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/clubworldcup/japan2011/matches/round=257437/match=300180901/index.html |title=Santos Futebol Clube vs FC Barcelona |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=18 December 2011 |access-date=4 March 2013}}</ref> a feat matched by the [[2023 FIFA Club World Cup final|2023 final]] twelve years later.
